1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Russian rabbi; born at Brest-Litovsk, where he died Aug. 13, 1837. Both his grandfather and father were rabbis there; he succeeded the latter in 1797, while still a young man, and held the position until his death.
2 CONC  He was considered one of the great rabbis of his time. He left no written works, but his name is found appended to a large number of approbations to works of contemporary authors. His signature is second on the resolution adopted
2 CONC  by the convention that assembled at Wilna in 1818 to select, by order of Alexander I., three Jewish deputies to reside in St. Petersburg as representatives of the Jews.
2 CONT
2 CONT See Unbroken Chain, Volume 1, Chapter VIII, page 585
2 CONT
2 CONT He was succeeded by his relative, R. Jacob Meir Padua (Unbroken Chain, 3rd Edition, Volume 3, page 350)
