2 CONT LANDAU:
2 CONT
2 CONT By: Gotthard Deutsch, Solomon Schechter, M. Seligsohn, Bernhard Friedberg, N. T. London, Herman Rosenthal, Richard Gottheil, Louis Ginzberg, Isidore Singer, Moses Löb Bamberger
2 CONT Table of Contents Eleazar ben Israel Landau: Ezekiel ben Judah Landau: Hermann Landau: Isaac Landau: Isaac Elijah ben Samuel Landau: Isidor Landau: Israel ben Ezekiel Landau: Jacob b. Judah Landau: Marcus Landau: Moses Israel Land
2 CONC au: Samuel ben Ezekiel Landau: A family name said to have been derived from the name of a city situated in western Germany.
2 CONT
2 CONT The name is found largely among Polish Jews, who probably were expelled from that city about the middle of the sixteenth century (see Löwenstein, "Gesch. der Juden in der Kurpfalz," p. 33, Frankfort-on-the-Main, 1895) and retained
2 CONC  the name in their new homes. The earliest bearer of it of whom there is record is Jacob Baruch ben Judah Landau, author of the ritual work "Agur," who lived in Italy about 1480 or 1490. From the latter part of the sixteenth centu
2 CONC ry the Landau family is met with in Poland, especially in the western part of Podolia, which, after the partition of Poland, was annexed to Austria. In various instances the name "Landau," which had become a Jewish family name, wa
2 CONC s adopted by people who had no family connection with the original emigrants from the German city. Thus, it was assumed by a great-grandson of Abraham ben Elijah Wilna (see Jew. Encyc. i. 107, s.v. Abraham ben Elijah of Wilna).
2 CONT
2 CONT The first known member of the Landau family in Poland is Ẓebi ben Moses Landau, one of the communal leaders of the Lemberg congregation, who died in Cracow Jan. 7, 1620 (Buber, "Anshe Shem," p. 186). Ẓebi ben Saul Landau was rabbi
2 CONC  of Zmigrod and died in Lemberg June 15, 1722. Solomon Landau, father-in-law of Jacob Joshua, lived in Lemberg toward the end of the seventeenth century (ib. pp. 195, 206).
2 CONT
2 CONT Only the above incomplete pedigree can be drawn of that branch of the family to which Ezekiel Landau belonged, and which had representatives in Zolkiev, Opatow, and Brody.
2 CONT
2 CONT The first member of this branch definitely mentioned is Judah Landau, who lived about the beginning of the seventeenth century. He and his son, Ezekiel Landau, are known only by name. The latter's son, Ẓebi Hirsch Landau, was a de
2 CONC legate to the Council of Four Lands, and was also one of the signatories to the privilege granted by that body to the printer of Zolkiev in 1699 (Buber, "Ḳiryah Nisgabah," p. 104, Cracow, 1903). One of his sons, Judah Landau, who 
2 CONC lived in Opatow, was father of the most famous scion of the family, Ezekiel Landau.
2 CONT
2 CONT Branches of the family live in Russian Poland and in Brody. Descendants of the same family are: Israel Jonah Landau (d. 1824), rabbi of Kempen, province of Posen, and author of "Me'on ha-Berakot" (Dyhernfurth, 1816), novellæ to th
2 CONC e Talmudic treatise Berakot; and his son, Samuel Joseph Landau (d. 1837), also rabbi in Kempen, and author of "Mishkan Shiloh" (Breslau, 1837), novellæ and responsa.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bibliography: Buber, Ḳiryah Nisgabah (on the scholars of Zolkiev), Cracow, 1903; Eisenstadt-Wiener, Da'at Ḳedoshim, St. Petersburg, 1897-98, passim. Pedigree of Landau Family. Eleazar ben Israel Landau: Rabbi of Brody, where he di
2 CONC ed of cholera in 1831. He was the author of a work entitled "Yad ha-Melek," novellæ on Maimonides' "Yad" and notes to the Talmud (parts i. and iv., Lemberg, 1829; part ii. ib. 1810).
