1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://wc.rootsweb.com/cgi-bin/igm.cgi?op=GET&db=lorenfamily&id=I72528 https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hirschel_Levin http://www.jewishencyclopedia.com/articles/9843-levin-hirschel-ben-aryeh-lob
2 CONT
2 CONT Rabbi Zvi Hirsch Berlin (also known as Hirschel Levin or the Reverend Hart Lyon). He was born in Rzeszow in 1721. In his youth he studied under his father and went with him to Amsterdam in 1740 when he was 19 years old. He develop
2 CONC ed knowledge of the Talmud, Hebrew Grammar, Jewish History, Philosophy, Physics and Geometry. He also took part in the Emden-Eybeschutz controversy, siding with Rabbi Emden, his maternal uncle.
2 CONT
2 CONT After his father died in 1755, he was elected to the post of Chief Rabbi of the Great and Hambro Synagogues of the Ashkenazi community in London, and accepted in 1758.
2 CONT
2 CONT In London he became known as the Rev. Hart Lyon and was painted by the famous artist Turner. He remained in London for 7 years and left because he was prevented from publishing a defense of Shechitah (Ritual Slaughtering). He beca
2 CONC me A.B.D. Halberstadt, and also head of the Yeshiva there until 1770, when he became A.B.D. Mannheim, and 3 years later A.B.D. Berlin.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was a close friend of Moses Mendelssohn and even wrote an approbation to his German translation of the Bible.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married
2 CONT  1.  Golda, daughter of Rabbi David Tebele HaKohen, who died in Berlin in 1794
2 CONT  2.  Shprintze (in 1797) when he was 78 years old. She was thedaughter of R. Abraham (of the family of the Chacham Zvi). After his death, some three years later, she remarried R. Zobel Eger A.B.D. Braunsweig.
2 CONT
2 CONT Levin supported and defended his son, Saul Berlin in the dispute in which he was involved, especially with the forged Responsa Besamim Rosh, published in Berlin in 1793.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the author of : Ateret Zekeinim, only in manuscript; Hagahot, in manuscript on Sefer Yochasin, Sefer Hachinuch, Kaftor ve-Perach, Sefatei Yeshamim;Minchat Ahava, published in Rotterdam in 1811; and a commentary on Mishna Le
2 CONC chem Shnaiyim(on Pirkei Avot), published together with his uncle R. Emden in Berlin 1834.
2 CONT
2 CONT A poem written by him can be found in the Binyan Ariel, by his brother R. Saul Lowenstam.
2 CONT
2 CONT =Source=
2 CONT
2 CONT The Unbroken Chain by Neil Rosenstein. Volume 11 G 8.2 p 821-822.
2 CONT http://www.loebtree.com/kloizner.html
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT ראה http://www.bil-is.com/story%20of%20a%20tombstone.html
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT LYON, HART (more correctly Hirsch Löbel or Lewin) (1721–1800), chief rabbi, born at Resha, Poland, in 1721, was son of Rabbi Arjeh Löb (1690–1755), by his wife, a daughter of Rabbi Lewi Ashkenasi, called Chacham Lewi. His father, 
2 CONC a well-known Jewish theologian, was rabbi successively of Resha, Glogau, and Amsterdam. At an early age the son distinguished himself by his knowledge of rabbinical literature, and wrote in 1751 with much vigour against Rabbi Jona
2 CONC than Eybeschütz, who was regarded as an adherent of the Polish Pseudo-Messiah, Sabbathai Zewi. After the death of Aaron Hart [q. v.] in 1756 he was elected chief rabbi of the London congregation of German and Polish Jews, and assu
2 CONC med office in the next year. He was known in this country as Hart Lyon. In 1760 there was published at Altona a Hebrew work by Jacob Kimchi, entitled ‘Shaalah-u-Theshouvah,’ in which the officers of Lyon's synagogue entrusted with
2 CONC  the duty of superintending the slaughter of animals by Jewish butchers were charged with neglecting the strict scriptural law. Lyon defended the orthodoxy of his officers, but the wardens of his synagogue refused him permission t
2 CONC o make a public reply to Kimchi's charges. Lyon consequently resigned his post in 1763, and accepted an offer of the rabbinate of Halberstadt. He was afterwards called to Mannheim, and ultimately to Berlin, where he was the friend
2 CONC  of Moses Mendelssohn, and where he died in 1800. He was both learned and witty. His name figures with that of his father and his son in ‘The Memorial of the Dead,’ which still forms part of the ritual of the chief London synagogu
2 CONC e. A manuscript containing the commentary of Gersonides (Ralbag) on Averroes, which Mendelssohn gave him in 1773, is preserved in the London Beth Hammidrash (Neubauer, Cat. No. 43), together with three manuscript volumes of rabbin
2 CONC ical ‘Responsa’ by himself (ib. Nos. 24–6). A portrait by Turner was engraved by Fisher.
2 CONT
2 CONT The son, called Rabbi Saul Berlin (d. 1790), published at Berlin ‘Mizpah yokteel,’ an attack on a learned Talmudical work by Rabbi Raphael Cohen, and a collection of rabbinical ‘Responsa,’ which he falsely pretended to print from 
2 CONC the manuscript of an early rabbi, Asher ben Jechiel. The fraud caused him to leave Berlin for London, where he died 19 June 1790 (Steinschneider, Catalogue, p. 2505).
2 CONT --------------------
