1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''From Scattered Leaves, http://www.scatteredleaves.co.uk/'''
2 CONT
2 CONT The Appelbaums were a typical middle class Jewish family, perhaps a bit nearer the Jewish faith than we were. Ilse’s father was somewhat gruff – a businessman, whose partner had cheated him out of money in their music publishing b
2 CONC usiness. They didn’t do badly though and even had a car for a while. As a travelling salesman, he also had an extramarital affair and fathered an illegitimate son, who turned up at the house years later – not to demand anything, b
2 CONC ut to make contact with the person he had learnt was his father! Ilse’s mother was a warm, cuddly sort of person – very friendly. She was intelligent, well educated, but was essentially a housewife.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1933, Ilse went to England with her brother, Kurt, and his wife Anna, the daughter of Ernst Cassirer – a very prominent philosopher, who had lost his job as a professor because he was Jewish, got offered an academic job in Swed
2 CONC en, and then at Imperial College London, and finally at Durham. 
2 CONT
2 CONT A few months after Ilse's arrival in England, her parents came over as well, soon after which her mother died of cancer. To leave Germany, even if you got a visa to come to England, you still had to pay the Jew Tax – a prohibitive
2 CONC  amount of money required by the Nazis on departure. I think this may have been waived in this case, due to Ilse’s mother’s illness. My parents, who remained in Berlin, saw them off. Once in England, my parents maintained contact 
2 CONC with Ilse’s father and they met occasionally and sometimes I went with them - so my ongoing connection with Ilse continued. She was at Bedford College at the time, part of the University of London, I think having got a scholarship
2 CONC . She got some income by teaching science students German, which they were only learning because they had to have a foreign language. She was always disgusted by their lack of interest in the subject! She then moved to Cambridge, 
2 CONC which had become Bedford College’s wartime base. On finding possible accommodation with an elderly landlady, Ilse felt it right to ‘admit’ to being Jewish. The landlady promptly replied: “Never mind, dear; our Lord was Jewish, too
2 CONC !” She got her PhD in German literature and got the highest mark London University had ever recorded. This enabled her to get a lectureship at University College.
