2 CONT
2 CONT ———————————————-
2 CONT
2 CONT Yitzchak Meir Alter
2 CONT
2 CONT Gerrer Rebbe
2 CONT
2 CONT Main work - Chidushei Harim
2 CONT
2 CONT Born	1794 (mentioned in his marriage certificate from 1812 as 18 years’ old)
2 CONT
2 CONT Magnuszew, Poland
2 CONT
2 CONT Died	10 March 1866
2 CONT
2 CONT Dynasty	Ger
2 CONT
2 CONT Successor: 	Chanoch Henoch of Alexander
2 CONT
2 CONT Wife:	Feigele Lipszyc
2 CONT
2 CONT Issue:Avraham Mordechai, Cyna Pesia
2 CONT
2 CONT Leah Hudes, Esther
2 CONT
2 CONT Yitzchak Myer Alter (also Rottenburg, Rothenburg or Rothenberg as an alternate for "Alter") (1798 – 10 March 1866), is considered to be the first Rebbe of the Ger Hasidic dynasty, which he founded in the town of Góra Kalwaria (kno
2 CONC wn as "Ger" in Yiddish), Poland. He was also known as the Chidushei HaRim for his Torah books. He is sometimes referred to fondly as Reb Itche Myer by his followers.
2 CONT
2 CONT Rabbi Yitzchak Myer was born in Magnuszew, Poland, in late 1798. He came from a very distinguished family of rabbis, among the most prominent in Germany and Poland. He was a descendant of Rashi and of the Tosafist, Rabbi Meir ben 
2 CONC Baruch of Rothenburg.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married Feigele Lipszyc, daughter of Moshe 'Halfon' Lipszyc, in 1811, and settled in Warsaw. They had fourteen children (according to most published sources), most of whom died in infancy.
2 CONT
2 CONT Rabbi Yitzchak Myer became known as a Talmudic genius. At first he was close to the rebbes of Kozhnitz. After some years he was drawn to the Rebbe, Reb Simcha Bunim of Prshischa, whose close adherent he became. After the demise of
2 CONC  the Rebbe, Reb Bunim, Yitzchak Myer became a disciple of Rabbi Menachem Mendel of Kotzk, known as the Kotzker rebbe, who was famous for his acerbic wit and Talmudic brilliance. He was soon followed by a large number of Reb Bunim'
2 CONC s followers. Rabbi Yitzchak Myer and he Kotsker eventually became brothers-in-law, when the Kotzker Rebbe married Chaya Lipszyc, sister of Rabbi Yitzchak Meir's wife Feigele.
2 CONT
2 CONT Rabbi Yitzchak Meir was succeeded as Rebbe by his colleague and disciple, Rabbi Chanoch Henoch of Alexander.
2 CONT
2 CONT One of his sons, Avraham Mordechai, and three daughters, Cyna Pesia, Leah Hudes and Esther, survived to adulthood and married. By the time of Rabbi Yitzchak Meir's death on 23 Adar 5626 (1866), only one child (Esther) remained ali
2 CONC ve.
2 CONT
2 CONT Works
2 CONT
2 CONT Rabbi Yitzchak Meir is also known for his talmudic commentary, though he wrote on many other areas. Extant published works are:
2 CONT
2 CONT Chidushei HaRim on Choshen Mishpat Part 1 Part 3
2 CONT
2 CONT Chidushei HaRim on Torah link
2 CONT
2 CONT Chidushei HaRim on Bava Batra link
2 CONT
2 CONT Chidushei HaRim on Bava Metzia link
2 CONT
2 CONT Chidushei HaRim on Bava Kama link
2 CONT
2 CONT Chidushei HaRim on Shavuot link
2 CONT
2 CONT Chidushei HaRim on Gittin link
2 CONT
2 CONT Chidushei HaRim on Ketubot link
2 CONT
2 CONT Chidushei HaRim on Pirkei Avot link
2 CONT
2 CONT Rebbes of Ger
2 CONT
2 CONT Yitzchak Meir Alter (1798-1866)
2 CONT
2 CONT Yehudah Aryeh Leib Alter (1847-1905)
2 CONT
2 CONT Avraham Mordechai Alter (1866-1948)
2 CONT
2 CONT Yisrael Alter (1895-1977)
2 CONT
2 CONT Simcha Bunim Alter (1898-1992)
2 CONT
2 CONT Pinchas Menachem Alter (1926-1996)
2 CONT
2 CONT Yaakov Aryeh Alter (b. 1939)
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources
2 CONT
2 CONT Meir Einei Hagoilo, by Avrohom Yisochor Binyomin Alter and Avrohom Mordechai Alter, (1928)
2 CONT
2 CONT Toldoth Horim, by Itshe Myer Broder of Ger.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Ger, or Gur (or Gerrer when used as an adjective) is a Hasidic dynasty originating from Ger, the Yiddish name of Góra Kalwaria, a small town in Poland.
2 CONT
2 CONT Prior to the Holocaust, Ger was the largest and most important Hasidic group in Poland. Today it is one of the largest Hasidic dynasties in the world, along with the Satmar dynasty. It is now based in Jerusalem and Bnei Brak.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Rebbes who lead the movement have the family name of Alter.
2 CONT
2 CONT The founder of this group was Rabbi Yitzchak Meir Alter (he changed his name from Rotenberg) (1798-1866), known as the Chiddushei HaRim after his primary scholarly work by that title.
2 CONT
2 CONT History of Ger Hassidim:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ger_%28Hasidic_dynasty%29
2 CONT
2 CONT ----------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT http://wiki.geni.com/index.php/Jewish_Dynasties
2 CONT
2 CONT ----------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT The Chidushei HaRim: Gerrer Chasiddus
2 CONT
2 CONT “I am certain that [Rav Yitzchok Meir] will reach the level of torah greatness of Rav Yehonoson Eibushitz”, so said the great gaon Rebbi Akiva Eiger after meeting with the Chidushei HaRim the future founder of the Gerrer Chassidus
2 CONC  for the very first time.
2 CONT
2 CONT Rav Yitzchok Meir was born in 5558/1798 to an orphaned mother who was raised by the Magid of Kozhnitz.  The Magid of Kozhnitz was a great influence on him in his younger years.  “When he was just 17 years old he published his firs
2 CONC t Chidushim and became known as the Illuy of Warsaw”. He then became a Chosid of Reb Simcha Bunim of Peshischa.  The Magid of Kozhnitz was hurt by this decision.  The Chidushei HaRim had thirteen children and they all died in his 
2 CONC lifetime.  He once said that he knows this was because he hurt the Magid of Kozhnitz but nevertheless he had no regrets.  He had no use for the royal treatment and compliments showered upon him in Kozhnitz.  He much preferred a Re
2 CONC bbe that would rip out his veins.
2 CONT
2 CONT After Reb Simcha Bunim’s death, although a possible successor in his own right, he submitted himself to his good friend Rav Menachem Mendel of Kotzk and became his follower and confidant.  They became brother in laws after Reb Men
2 CONC achem Mendel married the sister of the wife of the Chidushei HaRim after his first wife passed away.  After the Kotzker Rebbe was niftar in 5619/1859 the Chidushei HaRim moved to Ger outside of Warsaw and started his chassidus.
2 CONT
2 CONT He once said that "Rav Simcha Bunim Led his flock with Love, The Kotzker Rebbe instilled in his Chassidim Fear of Heaven, I will lead with Torah".  For a daily schedule he recommended as follows.  18 hours learning, four hours sle
2 CONC ep, and 2 hours for preparations, chassidus, davening, and eating.
2 CONT
2 CONT It is told that one morning Rav Shmuel Salant (the future Rav of Yerushalayim) came to visit the Chidushei HaRim.  After talking for a while in learning the Chidushei HaRim excused himself to go daven.  Rav Shmuel Salant shocked a
2 CONC sked him why he hadn’t daven yet.  He answered that he went to sleep late.  So then why, asked Rav Shmuel, did he not go to sleep earlier and wake up earlier.  To this he replied, when one is learning he doesn’t close the gemara a
2 CONC nd go to sleep.  Only when he falls asleep does he drag himself to a bed.
2 CONT
2 CONT In a difference in opinion with his close friend the Kotzker Rebbe, the Chidushei HaRim felt strongly about wearing traditional Chassidic garb even at the cost of one’s life. The zechus of not changing clothing in Mitzrayim proved
2 CONC  that this is the very essence of a Jew.  The Chidushei HaRim even ended up sitting in jail briefly over this battle with the authorities.  The Kotzker strongly argued with him finding no great value to externalities at all.
2 CONT
2 CONT After leading Ger for seven years he was niftar in 5626/1866.  With no children to take his place and his young grandchild and spiritual heir refusing to become Rebbe due to his young age all his Chassidim flocked to the Alexander
2 CONC  Rebbe.  Only upon the death of the Alexander Rebbe flock did his grandson Reb Yehuda Leib Alter (the Sfas Emes) agree to become Rebbe and continue the Gerrer Dynasty, responding to the pleas of a Rebbeless flock.
2 CONT
2 CONT Yehi Zichro Boruch  (based on Gedolei HaDoros by Rabbi YM Stern)
2 CONT
