1 OCCU Rabbi, head of Nemirov Yeshiva, and Martyr פרעות ת"ח, Av beit din Nemirov  http://www.hebrewbooks.org/pdfpager.aspx?req=39499&pgnum=1ראה גם: יוון מצולה, פרק ד-ה.  ומחבר הספר "שברי לוחות
1 TITL Rabbi
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Yechiel Michel ben Eliezer ha-Kohen (Hebrew: יְחִיאֵל מִיכל בֵּן אֱלִיעֶזֶר הָכֹּהֵן; died 10 or 12 June 1648), also known as the Martyr of Nemirov,[1] was a kabbalist and rabbi at Nemirov, Russia who was murdered 
2 CONC during the Cossacks' Uprising of 1648.
2 CONT
2 CONT Yechiel Michel was the son of the gaon Rabbi Eliezer of Zlatschov. Said to know the entire Torah by heart, Yechiel was known for his mastery of talmudic, kabbalistic, and secular knowledge.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Yechiel at first regarded the Khmelnytsky persecutions as a presage of the coming messianic era.[3] In a sermon on the Shabbat before the Cossack riots, he admonished members of the Jewish community to be martyred rather than forc
2 CONC ibly converted to Christianity.[4] When the hordes of Khmelnytsky, taking Nemirov, began the work of pillage and massacre, a Cossack concealed Yechiel, hoping that the latter would disclose where the Jews had hidden their wealth. 
2 CONC Yechiel was found by a Ukrainian shoemaker and clubbed to death in the Jewish cemetery on 10 or 12 June 1648.[2] He was mourned by Rabbis Shabbatai HaKohen and Yom-Tov Lipmann Heller in their elegies for the victims of the Khmelny
2 CONC tsky massacres of 1648–1649.[3][5]
2 CONT
2 CONT Yechiel was the author of a work entitled Shivrei luḥot ('Fragments of the Tablets'), containing kabbalistic commentary on several Sabbatic sections and the weekly Torah readings given in the Talmud. The work was published posthum
2 CONC ously at Lublin in 1680 by Yechiel's nephew, whose introduction includes Jewish accounts of the Cossacks' Uprising.[6][7] A new edition of the work was published by Rabbi Abraham Baruch Alter Rosenberg in 1913.[8]
2 CONT
2 CONT קשור לאילן טרויבע
2 CONT
2 CONT ------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.hebrewbooks.org/pdfpager.aspx?req=8597&st=&pgnum=471
2 CONT
2 CONT ------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Author of Shivrei Luchos: http://www.hebrewbooks.org/39499
2 CONT
2 CONT Note added by David Deutsch:
2 CONT
2 CONT Nemirov today is known as: Nemyriv, Nemyrivs'kyi district, Vinnyts'ka oblast, Ukraine
2 CONT
2 CONT I added this information in order to help modern researchers find this city on a modern map.
2 CONT -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.hebrewbooks.org/pdfpager.aspx?req=39499&pgnum=1
2 CONT
2 CONT Twentieth of Sivan
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1648, the Ukrainian nationalist and Cossack leader Bogdan Chmielniski incited a rebellion against Poland and Polish landlords who ruled over the Ukraine. Chmielniski's forces, combined with Tatar allies of Mongolia, routed the 
2 CONC Polish army at Yellow River on May 19, 1648, and Hard Plank on May 26. The Polish defeat was a disaster for the Jews of the Ukraine. They now faced a major catastrophe. Chmielniski's Cossacks attacked Polish forces and unleashed t
2 CONC heir fury against the Jews of the Ukraine and some surrounding areas, as well. Many Jewish communities were wiped out. Hundreds of thousands were murdered. The brutality of their attacks defies any description.
2 CONT
2 CONT Rabbi Nathan Nata Hanover, a contemporary chronicler of the events of this era known as Tach VeTat, an acronym for the Hebrew equivalent of the years 1648-1649, wrote about the Chmielniski pogroms in a book entitled Yeven Metzulah
2 CONC  (Abyss of Despair).
2 CONT
2 CONT The first reported attack against a large Jewish community was in the city of Nemirov. Jews from surrounding villages had gathered there from smaller villages for refuge. Just days earlier, on the Sabbath, the head of the city's y
2 CONC eshiva, Rabbi Yechiel Michael, son of Rabbi Eliezer, addressed the community and told them that if the enemy should arrive, the people must defy them if they demand baptism; even at the cost of their lives.
2 CONT
2 CONT As Cossack troops were nearing Nemirov, its Jews trembled in fear as they locked themselves within the city walls. As the soldiers drew nearer, they unfurled Polish flags to give the impression that they were Polish troops coming 
2 CONC to their rescue. The Poles within the city were notified of this ruse and collaborated with the Cossacks in order to save their own lives. They falsely informed the Jews guarding the gates that the approaching soldiers were indeed
2 CONC  Polish and that they should open the gates. As the Cossacks entered the city with drawn swords, slaughter ensued. Over six-thousand Jews were martyred on that day.
2 CONT
2 CONT From Nemirov, the Cossacks attacked the city of Tulshin, Polannoe, and then Ostrog and Zaslow. At each city, horrors followed. After less than two years fighting, the Tatar allies withdrew from the conflict, forcing Chmielniski in
2 CONC to a truce that lasted for eighteen months, followed by more violence that continued intermittently until Chmielniski's death in 1657.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the winter of 1650, rabbinic and lay leaders known as the Council of the Four Lands gathered in Lublin and declared the twentieth of Sivan, the day the city of Nemirov was attacked, as a day of fasting and commemoration for the
2 CONC  many martyrs of the Chmielniski pogroms.
2 CONT
2 CONT After Chmielniski's death, massacres by Ukrainian nationalists continued - a series of attacks in the eighteenth century, including the massacre of 1767-1768, in which the Jewish community lost tens of thousands.
2 CONT
2 CONT This is part of an article I saw on the Israel National News website. What interested me was that it was an excerpt from a book by Rabbi Nathan Neta Hanover Ashkenazi, a contemporary chronicler of the events of this era known as T
2 CONC ach VeTat, an acronym for the Hebrew equivalent of the years 1648-1649, he wrote about the Chmielniski pogroms in a book entitled Yeven Metzulah  יון מצולה (Abyss of Despair). This at least, a loose way to connect Rabbi Yechiel Mi
2 CONC chael, son of Rabbi Eliezer (מוהר"ר אליעזר) with his father
2 CONT --------------------
