0 @I6000000058544765175@ INDI
1 NAME Ernst Alexander /Appelbaum/
2 GIVN Ernst Alexander
2 SURN Appelbaum
2 _MARNM Appelbaum
2 NICK Ernst Alexander Appelbaum
2 NICK Ernie
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 8 MAY 1909
2 ADDR
3 CITY Berlin
3 STAE Berlin
3 CTRY Germany
1 DEAT
2 DATE 14 SEP 1971
2 PLAC Ealing, London, England
2 ADDR
1 OCCU
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''From Scattered Leaves, by Ilse's husband, http://www.scatteredleaves.co.uk/ '''
2 CONT
2 CONT Ernie, Ilse's younger brother, had started as an apprentice when the Nazis came and, with Ilse and his older brother Kurt, had also come to England in about 1936, courtesy of the Cassierers. He  wasn’t allowed to work in England a
2 CONC nd, in retrospect foolishly, decided that he would be better off with his parents back in Berlin. The moment he arrived in Berlin, he was picked up and taken to Sachsenhausen Concentration Camp. Ilse’s parents immediately let her 
2 CONC know about it. At the time, she was a student at Bedford College and was quite friendly with Professor Purdy, her German professor, whose good friend was the Vice Chancellor of London University. She told both of them what had hap
2 CONC pened and they asked to be kept informed if she found out what could be done. Ilse, living with the Montagu family, talked to a cousin of theirs, who was a senior civil servant. He went to someone in the Foreign Office for advice 
2 CONC and was told that there was one possibility – an Irishman who was very corrupt and good friends with Field Marshall Goring (who was flexible on racial matters – he once said “I decide who is a Jew”). He would intervene on the paym
2 CONC ent of something near £1,000 (probably equivalent to something like £100,000 now). Ilse told the Montagus and the Vice Chancellor, at which point the latter – most extraordinarily - pulled out a cheque-book and wrote a cheque for 
2 CONC £1,000 and gave it to Ilse. The payment was made and Ernie was released. He returned to England.
