1 NAME Dora (Dorottya) /Schreiber/
2 GIVN Dora (Dorottya)
2 SURN Schreiber
2 NICK Dora
1 NAME /וואהל/
2 GIVN
2 SURN וואהל
1 SEX F
1 BIRT
2 DATE 27 AUG 1900
2 ADDR
3 CITY Eger
3 CTRY Hungary
1 OCCU
1 TITL Dr.
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} birth record: https://www.familysearch.org/ark:/61903/3:1:33S7-9PC3-KQB?i=514
2 CONT
2 CONT Graduation as a doctor in the german university of Prague:
2 CONT https://is.cuni.cz/webapps/archiv/public/book/bo/1840355044744327/263/?lang=en
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.nli.org.il/he/newspapers/egy/1922/05/06/01/article/6/?srpos=1&e=-------he-20--1--img-txIN%7CtxTI-%22SCHREIBER+DORA%22-------------1
2 CONT Poorness during her studies.
2 CONT בהחלט. זהו מכתב עוצמתי מאוד, והתרגום לאנגלית משמר את השפה הגבוהה והדרמטית שהייתה נהוגה בעיתונות היהודית-הונגרית של התקופה.
2 CONT
2 CONT להלן תרגום מלא של המכתב ששלחה הרמין שרייבר למערכת עיתון "Egyenlőség", כפי שמופיע בצילומים שצירפת:
2 CONT
2 CONT Letter to the Editor regarding Dora Schreiber
2 CONT Published: April 1922
2 CONT
2 CONT To the Honorable Editor!
2 CONT I hesitated for a long time before taking this pen in my hand, for our dear, departed parents raised us—even in our poverty—with this principle: "As long as you have strength, do not ask; and your poverty need not be known by othe
2 CONC rs."
2 CONT
2 CONT Highly Honored Editor, I have a younger sister, a dear little sibling, who brings me to tears whenever she comes to mind. This is her second year studying as a medical student, and she is languishing in misery in Prague. She endur
2 CONC es this because she wants to become "someone" [a professional], to be different from her two older sisters, the teachers, who suffer for starvation wages.
2 CONT
2 CONT I can provide almost nothing for the little girl from my own wretched income, and when I read the articles in Egyenlőség, my nerves are shattered for weeks... I, who for three years now have served as both mother and father to my 
2 CONC siblings. The helplessness of being unable to provide for my "little flower," the sorrow that she must fight the same battle with hunger that I once fought—these are what move me to write.
2 CONT
2 CONT Noble Sir! I am not asking for a charitable donation. A loan—for the little medical student; a loan that she will repay as soon as she is finished [with her studies]. I am writing her address here: Dora Schreiber, med. stud. c/o A
2 CONC nna Zuchtowa, Prague, II. Na Moráni 7.
2 CONT
2 CONT This young girl is the paragon of kindness and diligence, and my only fear is that this prolonged starvation (koplalás) will grind down her constitution prematurely.
2 CONT
2 CONT Editor Sir, please help. I do not usually take our troubles to the marketplace, and this letter is my first cry for help, intended only for your ears—you, who wish to and are able to help.
2 CONT
2 CONT My God! If the child could only study here at home, perhaps a relative could support her with something; but with the "17-Krona" law—who is able to buy [a future]? Highly Honored Editor, the many terrifying blows that have rained 
2 CONC down upon us in recent years (illnesses, financial ruin, my father’s death) have only ruined our present; but the Numerus Clausus has ruined our future. I also have a younger brother; he graduated high school last year and has bee
2 CONC n drifting ever since, and who knows what will become of him?
2 CONT
2 CONT Editor Sir, please forgive my knocking on your door and do what you can.
2 CONT
2 CONT Nagykálló, April 2, 1922
2 CONT With deep respect, yours faithfully,
2 CONT
2 CONT Hermin Schreiber
2 CONT Unemployed State Teacher
