1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''[https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lazar_Brodsky Lazar Brodsky - Wikipedia]'''<br/>
2 CONT
2 CONT Born	Lazar Izrayilevich Brodsky <br/>
2 CONT September 7 [O.S. August 26] 1848 <br/>
2 CONT Zlatopol, Russian Empire <br/>
2 CONT Died	October 2 [O.S. September 19] 1904 <br/>
2 CONT Place of death: Basel, Switzerland <br/>
2 CONT Ethnicity: Ukrainian Jewish <br/>
2 CONT Citizenship: Russian Empire <br/>
2 CONT Occupation: Sugar industrialist, philanthropist <br/>
2 CONT Religion: Judaism <br/>
2 CONT Spouse(s): Sara Lurie <br/>
2 CONT Children: Maria, Vera, Klara, Margarita <br/>
2 CONT
2 CONT Lazar Izrayilevich Brodsky (Russian: Ла́зарь Изра́илевич Бро́дский, Ukrainian: Ла́зар Ізраїльович Бро́дський; September 7 [O.S. August 26] 1848 – October 2 [O.S. September 19] 1904) was a Ukrainian businessman of Jewish origin, su
2 CONC gar magnate, philanthropist and patron.[1][2][3] <br/>
2 CONT
2 CONT Lazar Brodsky was born in Zlatopol, a shtetl in Kiev Governorate of the Russian Empire, in the family of Jewish entrepreneur Israel Brodsky. Together with his brother Lev (Leon) he inherited his father's very successful sugar prod
2 CONC uction business.[2] He headed Alexandria Society of Sugar Mills, that controlled more than one fourth of the total sugar production in the Russian Empire. <br/>
2 CONT
2 CONT He was Member of the Board of the St. Petersburg International Commercial Bank, Director of the Board of the Kiev water facilities association, Managing Director and member of the Board of the Society of the steam powered flour mi
2 CONC lls, founder and the Chairman of the Board of the Second Steamship Company on the Dnieper River, Member of the Kiev Mutual Credit Society. <br/>
2 CONT
2 CONT Lazar Brodsky was widely known as philanthropist. He financed the Jewish hospital in Kiev and Jewish schools. He financed the construction of the biggest synagogue in Kiev, that later was named after him. The building was construc
2 CONC ted in 1897–1898, in Brodsky's estate.[1][2][3] <br/>
2 CONT
2 CONT Other city institutions established with his funds were the Bacteriological Institute and the Besarabsky Market. He was the initiator and one of the sponsors of the construction of the Polytechnic Institute in Kiev.[1][2][3] <br/>
2 CONT
2 CONT Lazar Brodsky supported the development of the tramway communications in Kiev. <br/>
2 CONT
2 CONT Lazar Brodsky died on September 19, 1904 from diabetes mellitus in Basel, on September 24 his body was brought to Kiev and the same day the funeral was organized at the Choral Synagogue. Brodsky was buried in a marble tomb at Luky
2 CONC anovka Jewish cemetery (the tomb has not survived); best people of the city attended the ceremony, including the governor, the mayor commander, and the mayor. According to Kiev newspapers, about 150 wreaths was sent for the funera
2 CONC l, ten of them were made of silver. <br/>
2 CONT
2 CONT Lazar Brodsky had four daughters but no sons. After his death his brother Leo inherited the dynasty. <br/>
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Awards and regalia'''
2 CONT * Portrait of Lazar Brodsky by Mykola Pymonenko
2 CONT * In 1900, at the Universal Exhibition in Paris, Brodsky received the French Legion of Honor for the high quality of the goods produced at his plants.
2 CONT * He was also a recipient of the Order of St. Vladimir
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
