1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Eintrag im »Gedenkbuch« des Bundesarchivs:
2 CONT
2 CONT * Levy, Rudolf
2 CONT
2 CONT * geboren am 15. Juli 1875 in Stettin / - / Pommern wohnhaft in Berlin
2 CONT
2 CONT * Emigration: 1933, Frankreich 1934, Italien
2 CONT
2 CONT * Deportation: ab Florenz Januar 1944, Carpi, Sammellager
2 CONT
2 CONT * Todesdatum: Januar 1944 Todesort: unbekannt
2 CONT
2 CONT cf.: https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rudolf_Levy
2 CONT
2 CONT &: http://blankgenealogy.com/getperson.php?personID=I1006&tree=Blank1
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Self-Portrait by Rudolf Levy, 1943. Museum Pfalzgalerie Kaiserslautern.
2 CONT
2 CONT Born into an Orthodox family in Stettin, Rudolf Levy was a central figure in the Munich and Paris avant-garde in the 1910s and enjoyed much success in the 1920s, particularly in the legendary gallery of Albert Flechtheim. The Nazi
2 CONC  seizure of power brought his career to an end.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1933 he fled to Rapallo, Italy, where he stayed with his friend and student, Bob Gesinus-Visser (1898-1978). In 1935, he went to Mallorca. After the outbreak of the Spanish Civil War, he went to New York to stay with his friend
2 CONC , the actor Erik Charell. He did not want to stay there, however, and went back to Europe; first to Dubrovnik, then Ischia, where he lived in an artists' colony. He supported himself with occasional painting sales and financial ai
2 CONC d from his family, including his now ex-wife, Genia.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1937, his works were placed on the Nazi list of Degenerate Art. After World War II began, his residence permit was cancelled and he had to leave Ischia. Charell worked hard to obtain a visa for him to return to the United State
2 CONC s or seek refuge in South America, but to no avail. In 1940, he was able to find a place to stay with friends in Florence. After the German Army occupied Italy in 1943, he had to go underground.
2 CONT
2 CONT In December of that year, he was lured into a trap by SS agents pretending they were art buyers. He was arrested by the Gestapo, briefly imprisoned at Le Murate, then placed on a transport to Auschwitz, where he was murdered.
