1 NAME Александра Соломоновна /Копельман/
2 GIVN Александра Соломоновна
2 SURN Копельман
1 SEX F
1 BIRT
2 DATE 1885
1 OCCU
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Mention of Sasha in Max Kopelman's memoirs (her brother):
2 CONT _____________
2 CONT After I was removed from the wet nurse’s breast, I came under the care of my Njanja, 
2 CONT Akulina Stepanowna Kutchenova, who was to stay with us for seven years. When my sisters 
2 CONT were born, Sonia in 1884 and Sasha, in 1885 she took care of them in turn. An amusing 
2 CONT detail: When Sasha’s birth was duly registered at the police station, it was discovered that 
2 CONT Sonia’s birth had never been properly recorded. The only remedy was to report the birth of 
2 CONT twins and so for many years the two of them went through school as twin sisters.
2 CONT _________________
2 CONT My sisters were growing up—Sonia in the meanwhile had graduated the Petri 
2 CONT School and was studying piano in Geneva, Sasha, the younger one, not wanting to submit to 
2 CONT the strict rules of the Petri School, was attending the Alexandrovska Gynasium, a school of 
2 CONT the Russian Ministry. The third one, Wera, was still studying at home; she was later sent to a 
2 CONT girls’ boarding school in Wiesbaden, Germany. My younger brother Fima 
2 CONT attended the Tenishev School, St. Petersburg’s most modern school.
2 CONT ____________________
2 CONT I was beside my self with joy, though I had expected nothing different, and set off for 
2 CONT Sima's house, which was at quite a distance. I had not prepared her and was afraid she would 
2 CONT be out, but I found her at home with her father. I took her aside and told her that I had not 
2 CONT been able to wait any longer and that my parents eagerly awaited her. We then went in to tell 
2 CONT her father that our engagement, that he had long suspected, was now official. We were about 
2 CONT to leave when her sister and brother-in-law arrived and there was much joy and 
2 CONT congratulations all around. When we arrived at my parents’, we found preparations in 
2 CONT progress—my favorite sister Sasha was ordering Champagne by telephone. Her joy was 
2 CONT particularly great, since Sasha was an intimate friend of Sima's and had kept our secret all 
2 CONT this time.
2 CONT _________________________
2 CONT About the middle of December, we were back in St.Petersburg. This winter was to be 
2 CONT a very joyous one in our house. My sister Sasha, who was studying languages in Brussels, 
2 CONT was secretly engaged to a very nice and very wealthy man, Sch. But at that time, Lev Jacub, 
2 CONT a chemist in a large Moscow sugar concern, young and very well built, appeared in our 
2 CONT house, bearing regards from someone or other in Moscow. He came back often, courted 
2 CONT Sasha and after a few weeks of this Sasha sent her regrets to Sch. and became engaged to 
2 CONT Jacub. This union, of which my parents never approved, was not to be a happy one and can 
2 CONT be said to have spoiled Sasha’s life. 
2 CONT So now there were two engaged couples in my parental home, and it was decided to 
2 CONT celebrate both weddings, back to back, in Wulka. The winter passed in a whirl of receptions 
2 CONT and celebrations. The daughter of M.Vishniak, Father’s bosom friend, was our guest that 
2 CONT winter. Sima and I frequently attended the Italian Opera, the Symphonic Concerts and were 
2 CONT often invited to parties. My mother was occupied, full time, with Sasha’s trusseau, and her 
2 CONT move to Moscow where she would live after her marriage. 
2 CONT _________________________________
2 CONT Sasha’s wedding was to take place on Sunday, the 23rd of June 1907, to be followed 
2 CONT by ours on Tuesday, the 25th. About one hundred guests were expected who—because of the 
2 CONT Sabbath—would arrive on the 21st and, of course, would not think of departing before the 
2 CONT 26th or the 27th.. Grandfather’s manager, Meller the machinist, man of a thousand magic 
2 CONT tricks, was abroad for weeks on various errands and the catering was placed in the hands of 
2 CONT an entrepreneur from Warsaw. From the nearest big city, Lublin, he ordered one hundred 
2 CONT beds with appropriate linen, blankets, etc.; guests were to be housed in all the buildings on 
2 CONT the estate. A special pastry chef and an expert in the manufacture of ices were hired. A 
2 CONT hundred kilo of live carp arrived from a nearby lake and kept alive in a reservoir built in 
2 CONT Grandfather’s hops factory. They were joined there by large, live salmons. Ducks and 
2 CONT chickens could be herded together from nearby farms, but all meat had to be imported from 
2 CONT Warsaw since the cattle in the area was not good enough. The orchestra also came from 
2 CONT Warswa. Last, but not least, the ceremony was to be conducted by Grandfather’s old friend, 
2 CONT a rabbi from Grodno.
2 CONT _______________________________
2 CONT 1926:
2 CONT In spite of our rather crowded living conditions and our limited budget, our home was 
2 CONT always open and became a refuge for Sima’s many friends. In those early years, we had the 
2 CONT possibility of having my sister Sasha and also Sima’s sister Basja with us from time to time. 
2 CONT Those were the last occasions to be together with those who were so near and dear to us—
2 CONT and particularly to Sima—before the Iron Curtain dropped and forever tore us apart from all 
2 CONT and everyone, we had left behind in Russia.
2 CONT (Gabrielle Kopelman's note here: My aunt Sasha Yakub returned to Russia, 
2 CONT at the urging of her sons who perhaps were Communists. And it seems from the foregoing, 
2 CONT so did Sima’s sister Basja)
