1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Obituary:  https://www.jewishpress.com/news/obituaries/rabbi-joseph-friedenson-yiddish-journal-editor-dies-at-93/2013/02/27/
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Joseph_Friedenson
2 CONT
2 CONT Joseph Friedenson (1922–2013) was a Holocaust survivor, Holocaust historian, Yiddish writer, lecturer, and editor of Dos Yiddishe Vort [yi].[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Friedenson was born in Łódź, Poland in April, 1922, to Rabbi Eliezer Gershon, an activist for Agudath Israel of Poland and the editor of the Beth Jacob Journal, curricula used throughout the Bais Yaakov movement in pre-World War I
2 CONC I Europe.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT When World War II broke out, Friedenson's family fled to Warsaw, eventually becoming prisoners of the Warsaw Ghetto where Joseph married Gittel Leah Zilberman of Szydłowiec. They were smuggled out of the ghetto and subsequently en
2 CONC ded up spending several years in the slave labor camp of Starchowicz, before it was liquidated.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT The family arrived at Auschwitz in July 1944. Joseph endured death marches, confinement in Ohrdruf and several other concentration camps, and was finally liberated from Buchenwald in April, 1945, by the United States Army.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Mrs. Friedenson remained in Auschwitz and was liberated by the Russian Army in early 1945. The couple was reunited several months later, spent several years in post-war Germany working to help other survivors rebuild their lives, 
2 CONC and immigrated to the United States in 1951 where Joseph joined Agudath Israel of America.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT He authored several books on the Holocaust, published by ArtScroll.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT He died in February 23, 2013.[2][3]
2 CONT
2 CONT His son-in-law is Rabbi Yosef Chaim Golding.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT ------
2 CONT
2 CONT Josef Friedenson
2 CONT * Ellis Island and Other New York Passenger Lists, 1820-1957
2 CONT * Birth:	Circa 1923 - Poland
2 CONT * Arrival:	1951 - New York, New York, United States
