2 NOTE Source: Lodz Chevra Kadisha records of the Old Cemetery (published by the Jewish Community in 1938)
3 CONT
3 CONT Translation: 2886 (P. Z. G.) Mojżesz Wajngold (Weingott), The correct form of the surname is: Weingott, but members of this family were often incorrectly called Wajngold or Wajngarł. Mojżesz Wajngold came from Kalisz, and his fami
3 CONC ly was proud of being descended in a direct line from the famous rabbi of Prague, the Maharal (5). Mojżesz Waingold was a Kock Hasid; he married the daughter of Katriel Lewenberg from Płock, about whom local tradition says that Na
3 CONC poleon stayed in his house when he spent the night in Płock during the Great War in 1812. M. Wajngold had three brothers: 1) Isachar, rabbi in Sompolno, and then in Częstochowa, author of the work rabbi: "פתחי שערים )Dyrhenfurt, 1
3 CONC 819), 2) Majer Wajn-gold (Weingott), resident of Zduńska Wola, a wealthy merchant, (cf. Friedman: History of Jews in Łódź, p. 137) and 3) Szymon in Ciechanów. Mojżesz Wajngold lived initially in Płock, then in Bodzanów near Płock,
3 CONC  and finally settled in Łódź. He left 8 children: 1) Gecla in Łódź, known under the nickname "געציל גרינער (Lajba, 3) Samuel Dawid, shochet in Mława, 4) Jakób, son-in-law of the rabbi in Łowicz, 5) Estera Malka, wife of Zyś Majzli
3 CONC c (Ostrołęka), 6) Krasa Liba, wife of Abraham Silberszac in Łódź (now in Jerusalem), 7) Sara, wife of Samuel Berman in Łódź, 8) Rywka, wife of Samuel Lejb Segal from Ostrołęka (now in Łódź).
