0 @N6000000003327772978@ NOTE
1 CONT G10.2. Meshullam Zalman of Vilna (Reb Zalman Uris), died 1836. He married  the daughter of R. Mordecai Zvi son of R. Isaac (Hamburger) Horowitz.  (See Horowitz Family Pedigree - Chapter XVI - G10.9/11.3)
0 @N6000000004339542079@ NOTE R' Baruch Mordechai was a son-in-law of R' Shmuel, Last Av Beis Din of Vilna.
1 CONT
1 CONT When R' Baruch Mordechai was 80 years old,  he travelled to Eretz Yisrael and is buried next to the Or Hachaim.
0 @N6000000004339542250@ NOTE FROM JEWISH ENCYCLOPEDIA:
1 CONT
1 CONT Father of Isaac Aaron; born 1804; died June 30, 1863, at Lemberg. He published in collaboration with his brother-in-law, Joseph Saul Nathanson, the following: "Mefareshe ha-Yam" (Lemberg, 1827), a commentary to the work of his unc
1 CONC le, Moses Joshua Heschel, rabbi at Tarnogrod, who had published a work on Baba Kamma under the title "Yam ha-Talmud"; "Me'irat 'Enayim" (Wilna, 1839; Zolkiev, 1842); "Magen Gibborim," on the Shulchan 'Aruk, Orach Hayyim (part 1, L
1 CONC emberg, 1834; part 2, Zolkiev, 1839); "Haggahot 'al ha-Shas" (printed in the Wilna Talmud ed.); "Ner Ma'arabi," annotations to the Jerusalem Talmud.
1 CONT
1 CONT After a collaboration of twenty-five years a difference arose between the
1 CONT two brothers-in-law, which was brought to a crisis by Rabbi Solomon Klüger's
1 CONT "Moda'ah le-Bet Yisrael" in reference to the baking of matzot by machinery,
1 CONT Rabbi Joseph Saul's answer, "Bishul Moda'ah" (1859), not meeting with
1 CONT Ettinger's approval. Ettinger even before this had commenced to work alone,
1 CONT publishing "Ma'amar Mordekai" to the Shulchan 'Aruk (Lemberg, 1852), and
1 CONT writing much that is still in manuscript.
0 @N6000000004339542067@ NOTE Author of Natei Avraham
0 @N6000000003640619412@ NOTE
1 CONT Rabbi in Grodno (Horodno) 1681-1683
0 @N6000000003640619403@ NOTE
1 CONT Rabbiner in Tykoczin und Brest-Litovsk, Oberrabbiner in Altona.
0 @N6000000003327767592@ NOTE
1 CONT G10.2. Meshullam Zalman of Vilna (Reb Zalman Uris), died 1836. He married  the daughter of R. Mordecai Zvi son of R. Isaac (Hamburger) Horowitz.  (See Horowitz Family Pedigree - Chapter XVI - G10.9/11.3)
0 @N6000000003327767600@ NOTE
1 CONT G11. Isaac Israel (Eizik Zalmans of Vilna), died 1858.
0 @N6000000003327792717@ NOTE
1 CONT G11.2. R. Aryey Lieb Katzenellenpogen succeeded his father at Brest and  held the rabbinical post until his death on August 13th, 1837, when he  was succeeded by his relative R. Jacob Meir Padua. (See Chapter II -  Section B.) He 
1 CONC left no written works,
0 @N6000000004339542425@ NOTE FROM JEWISH ENCYCLOPEDIA:
1 CONT
1 CONT Isaac Aaron Ettinger (also called Reb Itzsche):
1 CONT
1 CONT Galician rabbi and scholar; son of Mordecai Ze'eb Ettinger; born at Lemberg
1 CONT 1827; died there Jan. 16, 1891. Distinguished for his intellectual activity and industry, he was invited by several communities of Galicia to assume a rabbinate, but, being wealthy, he declined until, in 1868, he was persuaded to 
1 CONC accept the rabbinate of Przemysl. He had occupied this position less than two years, when the pressure of his private affairs compelled him to relinquish it and retire to Lemberg. His responsa exerted considerable influence; Mitna
1 CONC ggedim and Chasidim submitted to him questions of ritual; the thaumaturgic rabbis of Sadagora referred questions of inheritance to his decision; and he was regarded even by the government as the leader of the Galician Jews. He was
1 CONC  officially recognized by the Austrian minister of the interior as Nasi of Palestine, and as such he sent annually to Palestine about 50,000 gulden. When Zebi Hirsch Ornstein died in 1888, Ettinger was chosen rabbi of Lemberg, an 
1 CONC office which he filled until his death. A highly cultured man, his influence was felt also by the Reform party. He often appeared in the Polish city council to uphold his opinions.
0 @N6000000004339542442@ NOTE Wore Tallit and Tefillin all day until after Chatzot.
0 @N6000000003640619421@ NOTE
1 CONT Som enke levede hun de senere år hos sønnen Behrend Rothenborg i København, hvor hun døde d. 15 marts 1752. Hendes gravsten, stadig omkring 1920 godt bevaret, fandtes på kirkegården i Møllegade.
0 @N6000000004339542040@ NOTE R' Avraham was still a child when his father, R' Naftali Hirtz died.  He was brought up by his stepfather R' Yaakov Lichtenstadt, and hence he was also known as R' Avraham Liechtenstadt.
1 CONT
1 CONT He made his home both in Prague and in the city of Liechtenstadt, near Karlsbad.  He was an agent of Court of the King of Saxony, and Primate of Prague.
0 @N6000000004339542234@ NOTE Author of "Levush Mordechai"
0 @N6000000003327793991@ NOTE
1 CONT G8.5. Rebecca Rachel, died in Hamburg in 1735, married R. Jacob, A.B.D.  Tarnopol, son of R. Isaac HaLevi Landau. They were the parents of Reize  who married R. Isaac Hamburger Horowitz, A.B.D. Altona-Hamburg-Wandsbeck  (Chapter X
1 CONC VI).73
0 @N6000000004339542107@ NOTE Austrian rabbi; died in 1739; son of Eliezer ha-Levi Lichtenstein Ettinger.
1 CONT He succeeded his father in the rabbinate of Holleschau and in the directorate of its Talmudical school, which was at that time one of the most important in Moravia, and at which his brother, the author of "'Edut be-Yosef" (Sulzbac
1 CONC h, 1761), was a pupil. In 1717 Chayyim was appointed head of the Talmudical school of Lemberg, and in 1730 succeeded the author of the "Pene Yehoshua'" in the rabbinate of that place. Although Ettinger wrote several works and nume
1 CONC rous responsa, nothing was published under his own name. Only in the works of others, as, for instance, in those of his brother-in-law, Chayyim Cohen Rapaport, rabbi of Lemberg, may there be found a few scattered responsa and note
1 CONC s of Ettinger's, which give but slight indication of his Talmudical knowledge.
0 @N6000000004339542020@ NOTE AARON HA-LEVI OETTINGEN
1 CONT
1 CONT Galician rabbi; born about the beginning of the eighteenth century; died in Lemberg about 1769. He was one of a prominent family of rabbis, and officiated for the congregations of Javorov and Rzeszow. His father, Hayyim Judah Loeb
1 CONC  ben Eliezer, was rabbi of Lemberg, as was also his father-in-law, Hayyim Cohen
1 CONT Rapoport, author of responsa, ultimately published at Lemberg, 1861. Aaron
1 CONT strongly opposed the Chasidism which arose in Galicia, and especially
1 CONT attacked Elimelech of Lezaysk, the author of "No'am Elimelech". His approbations are found in various works of that period.
0 @N6000000003327783043@ NOTE
1 CONT G11.2. R. Aryey Lieb Katzenellenpogen succeeded his father at Brest and  held the rabbinical post until his death on August 13th, 1837, when he  was succeeded by his relative R. Jacob Meir Padua. (See Chapter II -  Section B.) He 
1 CONC left no written works,
0 @N6000000004339639087@ NOTE In 1877 his son was unable to pay the Box Taxpayers, and the record gives the father's name as Shloime.
