0 @N6000000003327798908@ NOTE
1 CONT G5.12. Chaya, from the first marriage, married R. Leib b. Abraham Ettinge (Vienna, mentioned above, G5.8). She died at Zolkiew in 1632.  She was  also married to Mordecai, son of Abraham Shrentzel Rappoport, whose brother Simeon m
1 CONC arried her first cousin, Miriam (G4.12/5.4. below).
0 @N6000000004339542186@ NOTE Rav Eliezer Halevi Ettinger is buried in Hollescahu in a Kever next to the SHaCH.
1 CONT
1 CONT The Dispute About Amulets.
1 CONT
1 CONT R' Yonatan Eybeschutz - German rabbi and Talmudist; born in Cracow about the year 1690; died in Altona Sept. 18, 1764. His father, Nathan (Nata), who was a grandson of the cabalistic author Nathan Spira, was called as rabbi to Elb
1 CONC enschitz, Moravia, about 1700, where he died about 1702 in early manhood (on the conflicting reports in regard to the date of his death see Dembitzer, "Kelilat Yofi," pp. 118 et seq., Cracow, 1888).
1 CONT
1 CONT Jonathan was then sent to the yeshibah of Meïr Eisenstadt, who was then rabbi of Prossnitz, and later to the yeshibah of Holleschau, where a relative, Eliezer ha Levi Oettingen, was rabbi. After the latter's death (1710) Eybeschüt
1 CONC z went to Vienna, where Samson Wertheimer intended to marry him to his daughter. He thence went to Prague, where he married Elkele, daughter of Rabbi Isaac Spira; and later on he resided two years at Hamburg in the house of Mordec
1 CONC ai ha-Kohen, his wife's maternal grandfather.
1 CONT
1 CONT About 1714 he returned to Prague, where he became preacher, probably in succession to Asher Spira, who died in that year (Hock, "Die Familien Prags," p. 381, Presburg, 1892). Here he soon became popular (see Nehemiah Reischer's le
1 CONC tter to Jacob Emden, in the latter's "Sefat Emet," p. 11b, Lemberg, 1877); but he also incurred the enmity of some of the family and admirers of the former rabbi, Abraham Broda ("Bene Ahubah," 15b; see Dembitzer, ib. p. 120a), amo
1 CONC ng them being Jacob Reischer, and David Oppenheimer, chief rabbi of Prague. These personal animosities were most likely responsible for the fact that about 1725 Jonathan was accused of sympathy with the followers of Shabbethai Zeb
1 CONC i, who were still very active.
1 CONT
1 CONT Jonathan took an oath that the accusation was false, and with the other members of the Prague rabbinate signed the excommunication of the followers of Shabbethai Zebi.
1 CONT
1 CONT Believing that his prospects in Prague were poor, he made an effort, upon the death of Jacob Reischer (1733), to secure the rabbinate of Metz. On this occasion he failed, but after Jacob Joshua, who had succeeded Reischer, had
1 CONT gone to Frankfort-on-the-Main, Eybeschütz again became a candidate, and was
1 CONT elected (1741). But in Metz, as in Prague, his congregation divided into enthusiastic admirers and bitter enemies. When in 1746 he was elected rabbi
1 CONT by the congregation of Fürth, the Metz congregation would not release him from his contract. In 1750 he became chief rabbi of Altona, Hamburg, and Wandsbeck.
1 CONT
1 CONT From that time he became a central figure in Jewish history. Shortly after his arrival in Altona a rumor began to spread that he still believed in the Messianic mission of Shabbethai Zebi. In substantiation of this charge a number
1 CONC  of "Keme'ot" were produced which, it was alleged, he had given to sick people in Metz and Altona, and the text of which, though partly in cipher, admitted of no other explanation than that given by his enemies.
1 CONT
1 CONT The inscription read substantially as follows: "In the name of Jahve, the God of Israel, who dwelleth in the beauty of His strength, the God of His anointed one
1 CONT Shabbethai Zebi, who with the breath of His lips shall slay the wicked, I decree andcommand that no evil spirit plague, or accident harm, the bearer of this amulet" (Emden, "Sefat Emet," beginning). These amulets were brought to J
1 CONC acob Emden, who claimed to have been ignorant of the accusations, although they had been for several months the gossip of the congregation.
1 CONT
1 CONT In his private synagogue, which was in his house, he declared that while he did
1 CONT not accuse the chief rabbi of this heresy, the writer of these amulets was
1 CONT evidently a believer in Shabbethai Zebi (Feb. 4, 1751). The trustees of the
1 CONT congregation, who sided with their rabbi, at once gave orders to close Jacob
1 CONT Emden's synagogue. Emden wrote to his brother-in-law, Aryeh LoÖb, chief rabbi of Amsterdam, and to various rabbis who were outspoken enemies of Eybeschütz, among them Jacob Joshua of Frankfort, Samuel Helman (Eybeschütz's successo
1 CONC r in Metz), and Nehemiah Reischer, rabbi of Kriechingen in Lorraine, formerly Eybeschütz's admirer, but now his bitterest enemy. All of these pronounced Eybeschütz a dangerous heretic, unfit to hold any rabbinical office.
1 CONT
1 CONT The Dispute About Amulets.
1 CONT
1 CONT However, the trustees of the Altona congregation declared Emden a disturber
1 CONT of the peace, against whom drastic measures should be taken; and the followers of Eybeschütz assumed such a threatening attitude that Emden was compelled to flee to Amsterdam (May 22, 1751). There he brought charges against his en
1 CONC emies before the Danish courts, with the result that the congregation of Altona was ordered to stop all proceedings against him. In Hamburg the conflict assumed such proportions that the Senate issued strong orders to make an end 
1 CONC of the troubles, which were disturbing the public peace (May 1, 1752, and Aug. 10, 1753; see "Allg. Zeit. des Jud." 1858, pp. 520 et seq.). Emden returned to Altona Aug. 3, 1752; and in December of the same year the courts ordered
1 CONC  that nothing should be published concerning the amulets. Meanwhile Eybeschütz's popularity had waned; the Senate of Hamburg suspended him, and many members of that congregation demanded that he should submit his case to rabbinica
1 CONC l authorities. "Kurze Nachricht von dem Falschen Messias Sabbathai Zebhi," etc. (Wolfenbüttel, 1752), by Moses Gershon ha-Kohen, a convert to Christianity, but a former disciple of Eybeschütz, was evidently an inspired apology.
1 CONT
1 CONT Emden and his followers, in spite of the royal edict, published a number of polemical pamphlets, and Eybeschütz answered in his "Luhot 'Edut" (1755), which consists of a long introduction by himself, and a number of letters by his
1 CONC  admirers denouncing as slanders the accusations brought against him.  His friends, however, were most numerous in Poland, and the Council of Four Lands excommunicated all those who said anything derogatory to the rabbi. A year af
1 CONC ter the publication of the "Luhot 'Edut" he was recognized by the King of Denmark and the Senate of Hamburg as chief rabbi of the united congregations of Hamburg-Altona-Wandsbeck. From that time on, respected and beloved, he lived
1 CONC  in peace. His enemy Emden testifies to the sincere grief of the congregation at the death of Eybeschütz ("Megillat Sefer," p. 208).
1 CONT
1 CONT Even the notorious extravagances and the subsequent failure in business of
1 CONT his youngest son, Wolf, seem not to have affected the high esteem in which
1 CONT the father was held.
1 CONT
1 CONT Eybeschütz's memory was revered not only by his disciples, some of whom,
1 CONT like Meshullam Zalman ha-Kohen, rabbi of Fürth, became prominent rabbis and
1 CONT authors, but also by those who were not under personal obligations to him,
1 CONT such as Mordecai Benet, who speaks in the most enthusiastic terms of him in
1 CONT his approbation to the "Bene Ahubah," and Moses Sofer, who tries to defend
1 CONT him in a case where he committed a very bad blunder (atam Sofer, Yoreh De'ah, No. 69). With regard to Eybeschütz's actual attitude toward the Shabbethai Zebi heresy, it is difficult to say how far the suspicions of his enemies wer
1 CONC e justified.
1 CONT On the one hand it can not be denied that the amulets which he wrote contain expressions suggestive of belief in the Messiahship of Shabbethai Zebi; but on the other hand it is strange that the accusations came only from jealous e
1 CONC nemies. Jacob Emden himself speaks of a rumor to the effect that even before Eybeschütz went to Altona he (Emden) had expressed himself in terms which showed a determination to persecute the successor of his father in the office o
1 CONC f chief rabbi ("Megillat Sefer," p. 176); and although he indignantly denies this rumor, he speaks in another place of the chief rabbinate of Altona as "the heritage of my fathers" (ib.p. 209).
1 CONT
1 CONT Eybeschütz's works, given in the order of their publication, are as follows:
1 CONT
1 CONT 1755. Luhot 'Edut. Altona.
1 CONT 1765. Kereti u-Peleti, novellæ on Shulhan 'Aruk, Yoreh De'ah. Altona. Taryag Mizwot, the 613 commandments in rimed acrostics. Prague.
1 CONT 1773. Tif'eret Yisrael, notes on the rabbinical laws regarding menstruation,
1 CONT with additions by the editor, Israel, grandson of the author and rabbi of
1 CONT Lichtenstadt.
1 CONT 1775. Urim we-Tummim, novellæ to Shulhan 'Aruk, Hoshen Mishpat. Carlsruhe.
1 CONT 1779-82. Ya'arot Debash, sermons, edited by his nephew Jacob ben Judah Löb
1 CONT of Wojslaw. Carlsruhe.
1 CONT 1796. Binah la-'Ittim, notes on the section of the "Yad" dealing with the holy days, edited by the author's disciple Hillel of Stampfen. Vienna.
1 CONT 1799. Hiddushim 'al Hilkot Yom-Tob, edited by Joseph of Troppau. It is in
1 CONT substance the same as the last-named work, but differs from it in wording, and contains in addition Maimonides' text. Both therefore present not a work of the author, but notes taken from his lectures. Berlin.
1 CONT 1817. Sar ha-Alef, novellæ on Shulhan 'Aruk, Orah Hayyim. Warsaw.
1 CONT 1819. Bene Ahubah, on the matrimonial laws in the "Yad," edited by his grandson Gabriel Eybescütz. Prague.
1 CONT 1825. Tif'eret Yehonatan, homilies on the Pentateuch (n.d., though 1825 is
1 CONT probably correct). Zolkiev.
1 CONT 1862. Perush 'al Piska Had Gadya, a homiletical interpretation of the "Had Gadya." Lemberg.
1 CONT 1869. Notes on the Haggadah, edited by Moses Zaloshin. Presburg.
1 CONT 1891. Shem 'Olam, letters on the Cabala, edited by A. S. Weissmann. Vienna.
1 CONT A commentary on Lamentations under the title "Allon Bakut," and homilies on
1 CONT the Pentateuch under the title "?eshe? Yehonatan," are extant in manuscript
1 CONT in the Bodleian Library (Neubauer, "Cat. Bodl. Hebr. MSS." pp. 50 et seq.).
