1 CONT G8.5. Rebecca Rachel, died in Hamburg in 1735, married R. Jacob, A.B.D.  Tarnopol, son of R. Isaac HaLevi Landau. They were the parents of Reize  who married R. Isaac Hamburger Horowitz, A.B.D. Altona-Hamburg-Wandsbeck  (Chapter X
1 CONC VI).73
0 @N6000000004339542456@ NOTE Author of Edut B'Yosef (Sulzbach, 1761)
0 @N6000000004339504296@ NOTE Av Beis Din Vilna (Per Shem Hagedolim Hashelishi).
1 CONT
1 CONT Russian rabbi; born about 1720; died 1793 at Wilna, where his father, who had been rabbi in Pruzhani, Rushany, and Wilkowyszky, spent his last years (see Abigdor ben Samuel). His father-in-law, Judah ben Eliezer, surnamed "Yesod,"
1 CONC  was probably the most influential citizen of Wilna in his time, and contributed much to Samuel's advancement. The latter was at first engaged in business; and several financial agreements between him and the "Kahal" (communal cou
1 CONC ncil) of Wilna (c. 1745) are recorded. In 1750, while still a young man, he was chosen rabbi of the old community of Wilna, but for several years his father-in-law administered the office, which, in those times of rabbinical juris
1 CONC diction over all secular Jewish affairs, was one of great responsibility. Later Samuel became rabbi of Smorgony, and there is a record of his having held also (c. 1777) the rabbinate of Königsberg (Epstein, "Geburot ha-Ari," p. 29
1 CONC , Wilna, 1870).
1 CONT In 1777 the Kahal of Wilna decided to remove Samuel from the rabbinate. The community was divided on the matter, and a quarrel ensued, which was conducted with much bitterness. The Gentile authorities also took sides, Prince Radzi
1 CONC will, the waywode of Wilna, agreeing with the views of the Kahal, which represented the secular authority of the Jewish community, while Archbishop Masolski took the part of the rabbi. Samuel and many of his partizans removed to t
1 CONC he suburb of Antokol, which was under the bishop's jurisdiction, in order to be safe from the persecutions of Radziwill, who ruled the city. Many were subjected to imprisonment or exile; and it may be said that both sides lost in 
1 CONC the end. The power of the ?ahal was broken, and under the new Russian dominion it was unable to regain its former status.
1 CONT Samuel remained rabbi in name only. The office died with him; and in the strict sense of the word Wilna has not since had a rabbi or "ab bet din." Samuel is not known to have left any writings; but he is mentioned in contemporary 
1 CONC rabbinical works with the highest respect.
1 CONT
1 CONT From Jewish Encyclopedia:
1 CONT
1 CONT From R. Samuel's time the title "rosh bet din" was discarded, no rabbi subsequently elected being authorized to assume that title; since then the rabbi has been called "moreh Zedek." The reason for the abolition of the title was a
1 CONC  quarrel in which R. Samuel was involved as a result of his having treated the community with disrespect.
